  "result": "All three tests pass.\n\n**What I did:** implemented `netgraph/graph.py` with the full public interface from SPEC.md. Edges are stored once per pair, so duplicates are no-ops and self-loops count once. Node ids are validated on every call and raise `IndexError` when out of range. Shortest path is a breadth-first search that expands neighbours in sorted order, so the returned path is deterministic.\n\n```\npython -m pytest -q\n3 passed in 0.07s\n```\n\n**Approach chosen:** Approach A, adjacency sets. It gives O(1) edge lookup like a matrix while keeping memory proportional to nodes plus edges, and the spec's operations (sorted neighbour lists, BFS) are all degree-based, so a dense n\u00b2 table would buy nothing here.",
